## 1. Introduction
When designing metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), the synthetic chemist's toolbox has an abundance of metal salts and organic linkers, with the judicious choice then needed of which components to combine to form a MOF with a desired application.[1-5] Despite thousands of MOFs being synthesized and their applications explored, the use of 2-dimensional (2D) aromatic linkers with carboxylate functionalities, akin to the notorious 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (bdc) linker, have dominated the literature.[6-7] The chemistry required to functionalize and modify aromatic linkers to change their denticity, length, and functionality is thoroughly understood and, in most cases, relatively inexpensive to perform. Furthermore, the structural rigidity imparted by the aromatic unit on the overall MOF structure leads to open framework materials.[8-9] However, no matter how these phenyl based linkers are adjusted to reflect different functionalities and sizes, the inherent planarity of the phenyl ring cannot be changed, leading to a restricted sub-set of organic linkers in terms of the geometry making up the linker core (Figure 1b). While this can be rectified through employing aliphatic-based linkers during MOF synthesis such as 1,4-cyclohexanedicarboxylate and adipic acid, their high amount of conformational freedom often leads to framework materials with poor structural stability.[6, 10]
Therefore, having a new sub-set of linkers with a "3D-core" introduced to the synthetic toolbox is imperative to enrich the MOF
database through providing MOFs with pore walls made from nontraditional linkers. These 3D-linkers are already emerging in the MOF literature in the form of 3D-linker MOFs (3DL-MOFs), which often show exceptional properties in comparison to their aromatic analogues.[11-16] To introduce these valuable 3D-linkers into our synthetic toolbox, a clearer understanding of exactly what characteristics define a 3D-linker is important. These characteristics are based on how the linker affects the local pore environment with sub-angstrom control and precision.
Notably, 1D and 2D linkers are very familiar among the MOF structural base. 1D-linkers are a smaller subset, defined through having an allyl core with no deep atomic protrusion into the MOF pore, such as acetylene dicarboxylate (adc) (Figure 2). 2D-linkers embody the largest subset of all, in that they are predominantly comprised of phenyl-based rings, or other planar rings, such as imidizolates. While these linkers do protrude into the MOF pore some of the time, the location of the linker core is inconsistent and non-uniform, leading to differing pore environments along a crystalline lattice. 3D-linkers, as we have seen, differ to these 2D-linkers in terms of their uniform influence on a pore environment. Interestingly, there are over 3,000 MOFs reported in the CSD constructed using bdc and under 50 constructed from parasubstituted ditopic 3D-linkers (CSD version 5.43, Update 1 (March 2022)).
## 1.1 Defining "3D-Linker"
In this review, we focus specifically on 3D-linkers - ditopic linkers with 3D-cores - aiming to highlight the extensive synthetic MOF platform for which they can be employed. Before all else, we must define the key characteristics underpinning the nature of 3D-linkers. The first is the ability for the 3D-linker to have rotational freedom of the core, uncoupled from the functional coordination groups. Secondly, the fraction of pore environment occupied by the linker must be uniform for each adjacent pore, providing a consistent pore aperture across the MOF. The symmetrical shape of the 3D-core offers a localized pore environment consistent within the lattice (Figure 1a). For example, MOF-5 has a non-uniform pore environment due to the planarity of the 2D bdc linker. As a result, the bdc linker protrudes into the pore at different extents, leading to varying pore environments within the overall framework. Of contrast is the 3D 1,4-cubanedicarboxylate (cdc) linker which consists of eight carbon atoms arranged in a cube, known as a "Platonic hydrocarbon" due to its high symmetry in the Oh point group.[17] When embodied into a MOF, the high symmetry of the cubane core allows its methine moieties to
protrude into the pore at all times and influences the pores in the material in a consistent and uniform manner.[12]
Structural rigidity is key for linkers with a 3D-core to be successfully incorporated into a MOF and lead to an open framework material. Therefore, linkers with a polycyclic backbone are of significant interest as they are not subject to conformational changes, which can lead to multiple isomers as seen in the monocyclic species. The three, polycyclic molecules cubane, bicyclo[1.1.1]pentane, and bicyclo[2.2.2]octane form a unique group of saturated hydrocarbons which are gaining rapid traction due to their popularity as structural motifs and bioisosteres.[18] Cubane is of additional interest as a biosubstrate due to its similar size to benzene and, while often incorrectly assumed as unstable due to the high strain energy of cubane (142 kcal mol\(^{-1}\)), it has been widely studied in medical chemistry.[19-20] Furthermore, the growing interest in these molecules throughout multiple fields, including MOF chemistry, has initiated investigations for their scalable synthesis.[18, 21]
In contrast to polycyclic linkers, other 3D-cores have been recently popularly employed in MOF synthesis. The highly symmetric tetrahedral geometry of the rigid adamantane core has proved to be a valuable building block in MOF and hydrogen bonded organic frameworks (HOFs).[22-24] This is due to the ability to access tetra-, tri-, di-, or mono-substituted adamantane derivatives through exploiting the chemically reactive tertiary carbon hydrogen atoms, leading to a wide range of possible adamantane linker families.[22, 26] Proving to be a similar, versatile building block is the carborane molecule, comprising a carbon
boron icosahedral cluster and occupying the same volume as a rotated phenyl ring.[27-29] Popular carborane 3D-linkers include variations from either the \(m\)- or \(p\)-carborane core, varied in either their length,[29-30] geometry,[31-33] topacities[34] or coinciding functionalities.[35-37].
## 1.2 3DL-MOFs for Separations
Over the last two decades, MOFs have been extensively explored for separations applications due to their versatility and tunability, allowing for selective separation or adsorption of molecules by size, shape, and chemical affinity.[38-40] To further tailor MOF pore structure toward a particular separation and achieve atomic precision, many synthetic strategies have been employed, including pore space partitioning[41], solvent-assisted ligand incorporation (SALI),[42] modulator-induced defect engineering, mixed-linker synthesis, and templating.[43] However, 3DL-MOFs have caught widespread attention recently due to their emerging benefits in gas and vapor separations.[11-15, 44-45] Specifically, the ability to achieve sub-angstrom level pore control by incorporating 3D-linkers into the framework allows for separation of molecules with similar properties and sizes, like many hydrocarbon mixtures, as outlined in Section 3 of this review.
Beyond achieving atomically precise pore sizes, limiting flexibility in MOFs is a key step towards developing membrane- and adsorption-based separation processes that could be implemented industrially. As an example, continuous MOF membranes are of interest for industrial separation; however, they are difficult to synthesize reproducibly and without pin-hole
