## 2. Structural investigations
The first purposeful introduction of a \( p \)-dicarboxylate 3D-linker within a MOF was that of 1,12-dihydroxycarbonyl-1,12-dicarbacyclosododecaborane (\( p \)-carborane \( = p \)-CB) by Farha and coworkers (2007).[11] The \( p \)-CB linker is a 12-vertex icosahedral, ditopic carboxylate linker with similar metrics as bdc and exhibits excellent thermal and chemical stability.[48] While an isoreticular analogue of MOF-5 was aimed for using \( p \)-CB to generate a MOF with smaller pores and higher heat of adsorption, a more complex MOF was formed: \([Z\mathsf{n}_{3}(\mathsf{O H})(p\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C B})_{2}\mathsf{S}_{5}(\mathsf{D E F})_{4}]\) (1) \((\mathsf{D E F} = \) diethylformamide). Single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) of 1 reveal DEF molecules coordinated octahedrally to two of the three Zn atoms, and while these could be removed with thermal activation the MOF was irreversibly altered and exhibited a decrease in the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area from about 250 and \( 150\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1} \). Nevertheless, 1 exhibited impressive \( \mathsf{H}_{2} \) uptake of \( 2.1\ \mathsf{w t}\% \) and isotheric heats of adsorption, attributed to either reduction of pore size or the exposure of open metal sites upon solvent removal (Figure 3).
Using this research as a platform, the Northwestern team (2009) then developed some structurally interesting cobalt(II) \( p \)-CB MOFs, distinguished by their morphology and porosity.[49] Through altering the reaction conditions while keeping the metal ion and linker constant, three different MOFs were formed, the most interesting case being \([C o_{4}(O H)(p\mathrm{-}C P)_{3}D M F_{2}]\) (2) which exhibited a high BET surface area of \( 1030\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1} \) with a pore size distribution (PSD) of \( 4.9\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}} \). Gas adsorption studies of 2 revealed
a high \( \mathsf{H}_{2} \) uptake of \( 181\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1} \).[49] Further structural studies using \( p \)-CB as an organic MOF linker emerged from Jin and co-workers (2012), where a series of lanthanoid \((\mathsf{L}\mathsf{n}^{3+})\) microporous coordination polymers was produced using nitrate salts of \( \mathsf{Y b}^{3+} \), \( \mathsf{Y}^{3+} \), \( \mathsf{T b}^{3+} \), \( \mathsf{G d}^{3+} \), \( \mathsf{E u}^{3+} \), \( \mathsf{S m}^{3+} \), \( \mathsf{C e}^{3+} \) and \( \mathsf{L a}^{3+} \). Interestingly, while each \( \mathsf{L n}^{3+} \) exhibits an octahedral coordination environment, the amount of coordinated \( p \)-CB is reliant upon the size of the \( \mathsf{L n}^{3+} \) and elegantly follows the lanthanoid contraction trend.[50]
## MINIREVIEW
An additional advantage of 3D-linkers is seen in the chemical bonds connecting the 3D-core to the coordination functionalities. This was recently made aware by Comotti and Sozzani and coworkers (2020) through isolating bicyclo[1.1.1]pentane-1,3-dicarboxylate (pdc) in an IRMOF framework and observing its rapid rotational dynamics in comparison with bdc.[51] The soft torsional energy barriers between the \(s p^{2}\) carbon atom of pdc connecting to the \(s p^{2}\) carboxylate carbon, allows the bicyclo[1.1.1]pentane to rotate free from the carboxylate functionalities. This rotational behaviour was further explored by Macreadie and co-workers (2023) through investigating the influences of core rotation on material negative thermal expansion (NTE). Variable temperature powder X-ray diffraction (VT-PXRD) data of CUB-5 \((([\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}(\mathsf{c d c})_{3}])\) (3) and 3DL-MOF-1 \(([Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0(\mathsf{p d c})_{3}])\) (4) was used to calculate the linear coefficient of thermal expansion (CTE) and highlighted the enhanced NTE characteristics of both 3 and 4 compared to MOF-5. Using DFT calculations, the freedom of rotation of the 3D-linker cores were calculated and highlighted the additional vibrational pathways to lattice movement made available from the 3D-linkers (Figure 4). This consequently lowered the vibrational energy landscape needed to induce NTE for the IRMOFs.
## 3. Separation and Adsorption Investigations
## 3.1. Light hydrocarbon separations
Thus far, 3DL-MOFs have showed excellent performance in the separation and/or selective adsorption of light hydrocarbons (LHs) (ie. C1 – C9 hydrocarbons).[13, 52] As a critical feedstock to produce widespread chemical commodities, the separation of these LHs during petrochemical or natural gas processing is notoriously difficult and highly important.[53] Common separation by distillation and extraction methods are becoming phased out due to their poor cost-to-benefit ratios and high energy footprint, and adsorption separation processes are now preferred.[53]
In recent years, natural gas production in the United States has peaked, producing \(23.1\%\) of the global supply in 2021.[54] Natural gas is primarily composed of methane \((\mathsf{C H}_{4})\) but can have dilute
## MINIREVIEW
amounts of other hydrocarbons like ethane \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right)\) and propane \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right)\), which need to be removed before it is consumed.[55] After removal, \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) can be used as fuels or precursors for alkene and polymeric chemical products.[56] Because the quantities of ethane and propane in natural gas streams are ultralow, they are difficult to separate from methane by conventional distillation methods.[53] Therefore, membrane- or adsorption-based separations using porous materials, like MOFs, are promising alternatives to distillation, as they require less thermal energy input. Yang and co-workers (2022) demonstrated the efficacy of employing 3DL-MOFs for this separation.[15]
Two 3DL-MOFs were synthesized, [Al(OH)(pdc)] (5) and [Al(OH)(cdc)] (6), and structurally characterized using PXRD to exhibit wine-rack topologies with pore apertures of \(4.33\mathrm{~\AA~}\) and \(5.35\mathrm{~\AA~}\), respectively. Both frameworks are isostructural to the flexible MIL-53(Al) MOF and the permanently porosity of 5 and 6 was confirmed using Ar adsorption to give BET surface areas of 504 and \(462\;\mathsf{m}^{2}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), respectively.
To probe the materials' ability to separate the heavier hydrocarbons \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\right)\) from \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\) in a natural gas stream, single-component adsorption isotherms for \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\), \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\), and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) at \(273\ \mathsf{K}\), \(298\ \mathsf{K}\), and \(313\ \mathsf{K}\) were performed (Figure 5). At all temperatures, both MOFs exhibited steep uptakes of \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) compared to \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\), due to van der Waals interactions between the heavier hydrocarbons and the hydrogens in the 3D-linker. Both frameworks had the highest affinity for \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) (2.72 and \(2.52\,\mathsf{m m o l}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) for 5 and 6, respectively); however, they were able to accommodate more molecules of \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) (2.95 and 2.82 mmol \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), respectively) due to its shorter length. The authors observed higher overall uptake and steeper adsorption isotherms for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) in 6 than 5, indicating that 6 had stronger interactions between the framework and the \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) molecules, which can be attributed to greater pore confinement brought about by
## MINIREVIEW
the cdc linker. MOF 6 exhibited Ideal Adsorbed Solution Theory (IAST) selectivity values of 5:85 for \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8};\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}\) and 10:85 for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6};\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{4}\), setting a new benchmark for \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) separations in porous materials. To further evaluate the potential for \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) separation over 5 and 6, the authors conducted breakthrough experiments. In both cases, the frameworks showed little to no uptake of \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\), while \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) were retained in the MOFs. The retention times for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) were 146 and \(535\;\mathsf{m i n}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) in 5 and 318 and \(742\;\mathsf{m i n}\;\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) in 6. Through employing 3D-linkers, the accessible pore space is reduced, enhancing the interactions between \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) molecules and the framework.
Yang and co-workers (2023) also demonstrated that incorporation of 3D-linkers can be an effective strategy for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}\) separation.[45] Two isostructural pirpared-layer 3DL-MOFs, [Ni(pdc)(ted)1/2] (7) and \([\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{p d c})(\mathsf{t e d})_{1/2}]\) (8), were synthesized using mixed 3D-linkers; pdc and 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane (ted). Interestingly, the ted imparts additional flexibility in the MOFs which transition from an orthorhombic lattice to a tetragonal lattice when the MOFs are activated (Figure 6). These structures provided more accessible \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) binding sites which allowed for more van der Waals interactions with guest molecules compared
with the aromatic analogues. Furthermore, the structures of 7 and 8 consisted of pore sizes comparable to \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) which, in addition to the presence of carboxylate functionalities at the corner sharing nodes, led to a selective uptake for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}\).
## 3.2. Hexane isomer separations
Installing 3D-linkers has also been recognized as an effective strategy for the separation of hexane isomers (n-hexane = nHex; 2MP = 2-methylpentane; 3MP = 3-methylpentane; 2,3-dimethylbutane = 23DMB; 2,2-dimethylbutane = 22DMB). Separating these components is particularly difficult due to the similar boiling points (nHex = 69 °C, 2MP = 60 °C, 63 °C, 23DMB = 58 °C, and 22DMB = 50 °C) and kinetic diameters of these molecules (nHex = 4.3 Å, 2MP = 5.0 Å, 3MP = 5.0 Å, 23DMB = 5.8 Å, and 22DMB = 6.2 Å). The di-substituted isomers (22DMB and 23DMB) have higher octane values as compared with the linear and mono-branched isomers, making them more desirable for use as efficient fuels that are resistant to spontaneous ignition in an engine.[57]
## MINIREVIEW
Upon separation of the di-substituted the linear and monosubstituted molecules can be returned to an isomerization reaction to produce more of the higher value di-substituted isomers. However, precise, sub-angstrom level control over the pore size of the porous media is required to achieve separation of hexane isomers due to their similar properties.
Farha and co-workers have published two studies where they achieved precise pore control of MOFs by installing 3D-linkers and demonstrated their capabilities to selectively separate hexane isomers by size and shape. In their first study, a 3D-linker analogue of MIL-53(Al) was synthesized through substituting pdc for bdc as the organic linker component in MIL-53(Al) giving [Al(OH)(pdc)] (NU-2002 (5)) (Figure 7a).[58] Installation of the 3D-linker resulted in a reduction of the structural flexibility to \(\sim2\%\) between \(105~\mathsf{K}\) and \(398~\mathsf{K}\) using X-ray diffraction studies (Figure 8a). In contrast, MIL-53(Al) shows a \(\sim40\%\) increase in unit cell volume between \(100~\mathsf{K}\) and \(450~\mathsf{K}\).[59] Furthermore, SCXRD studies of host-guest interactions, showed that adsorption of hexane isomers did not result in significant changes in the unit cell parameters, which lends to the rigidity of the structure.
Single-component adsorption isotherms at 298 K exhibit adsorption of the hexane isomers in 5 in order of decreasing size \((n\mathsf{H e x}>2\mathsf{M P}>3\mathsf{M P}>23\mathsf{D M B}>22\mathsf{D M B})\) in the low-pressure region (Figure 7c). Grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations were also used to confirm the order of adsorption in 5 and IAST was used to calculate adsorption and selectivity in an equimolar mixture from the experimental isotherms at \(298~\mathsf{K}\), which demonstrated selective adsorption of linear and mono-branched isomers \((n\mathsf{H e x}+2\mathsf{M P}+3\mathsf{M P})\) over the di-branched isomers (23DMB+23DMB) (Figure 7b).
This advocates 5 as a promising candidate for hexane isomer separation. Additionally, the isomer adsorption in 5 was compared to the adsorption in MIL-53(Al), to evaluate the effect of increasing linker dimensionality from 2D to 3D. MIL-53(Al) showed no significant difference in adsorption of the different isomers in the low-pressure region, unlike in 5, indicating that implementing the 3D-linker improves rigidity and hexanes isomer separation performance.
In the second study from Farha and co-workers, a zinc-based MOF was synthesized containing 1,4-bicyclo[2.2.2]octanedicarboxylate (bdoc), ([Zn(bdoc)] (NU-2200, 9), which demonstrated impressive thermal stability and separation capability toward hexane isomers (Figure 7b, e-f).[60] This MOF demonstrated impressive differentiation of hexane isomers based on single-component adsorption isotherms which was further corroborated using multicomponent batch liquid uptake experiments.
## 3.3. Benzene/cyclohexane separations
Separating benzene (Bz) and cyclohexane (Cy) is extremely challenging for the petrochemical industries due to their similar molecular geometries, boiling points (353.25 K and 353.85 K respectively), and their propensity to form azeotropic mixtures.[53]
## MINIREVIEW
61] These properties cause distillation processes to be energy-intensive and expensive, and therefore separation processes using adsorption techniques have recently become widely explored.[53] While there exist synthetic strategies to alter the MOF pore environment to invoke selective adsorption of benzene over cyclohexane (Bz/Cy) or cyclohexane over benzene (Cy/Bz), the advent of separations using 3DL-MOFs has recently proved to be an effective strategy.
The effectiveness of 3DL-MOFs to selectively adsorb either Bz or Cy was first observed in a study performed by Macreadie and Hill and co-workers (2019).[12] Using the cubane 3D-linker, CUB-5 \((\mathsf{[Z n_{4}O(c d c)_{3}]})\) (3) was synthesized, an analogue of MOF-5 \((\mathsf{[Z n_{4}O(b d c)_{3}]})\) . The length of the coc and bdc linkers are comparable and reflected in the crystallographic cell dimensions of 3 and MOF-5, 12.73 Å and \(12.83\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) respectively. Significantly, the framework pore volume of 3 and MOF-5 (1.2 and \(0.92\,\mathsf{c m}^{3}\,\mathsf{g}^{-}\)
respectively) and surface area (3625 and \(3107\ \ m^{2}\ \ g^{-1}\) respectively) are considerably different. These differences are a result of the methine groups of the cubane core protruding into the pore of 3 and, due to the 3D geometry of the cubane, these methine groups extend into the pores in a uniform manner and cause the pore to have a contoured shape (Figure 9). Gas and vapor adsorption measurements show 3 to adsorb \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) , \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) and Bz at lower pressures compared with MOF-5, with the overall amount adsorbed to be higher in MOF-5 due to the higher surface area. To understand the higher adsorption of Bz in 3, DFT-D3 calculations were performed to reveal a Bz-binding energy of \(-49.5\ \ \mathsf{k J\ \ m o l^{-1}}\) and \(-42.5\ \ \mathsf{k J\ \ m o l^{-1}}\) within 3 and MOF-5 respectively. These calculations were corroborated by further simulation studies which highlighted numerous close interactions between the 3 contoured pore environment and Bz compared with MOF-5.
Macreadie and co-workers (2021) then extended this research to explore the ability of each MOF to separate an equimolar mixture of Bz and Cy under dynamic conditions using the breakthrough separations, while also exploring a new IRMOF, 3DL-MOF-1, constructed using the 3D-linker with the bicyclo[1.1.1]pentane core \((\mathsf{[Z n_{4}O(c d c)_{3}]})\) (4).[52] The 3D geometry of the pdc linker also causes 4 to exhibit a contoured shape with a smaller surface area \(2378\ m^{2}\ g^{-1}\) . Interestingly, both 3 and 4 are not interpenetrated due to the bulkiness of the linker, and the short length of the pdc linker causes 4 to be the smallest IRMOF synthesized with accessible pore spaces. The IAST was used to calculate selective adsorption of Bz/Cy from 0 to \(5\,\mathsf{k P a}\) , with both 3 and 4 exhibiting higher selectivity or Bz/Cy compared to MOF-5. This interesting result prompted further investigation into their behavior under dynamic breakthrough conditions, highlighting the superb selectivity for MOF-5 to adsorb Cy/Bz and 3 to adsorb Bz/Cy (Figure 10). These experiments emphasize the importance of supramolecular host-guest interactions in separating this difficult
mixture, particularly due to the aromatic MOF-5 framework selectively adsorbing the aliphatic guest molecule.
## 3.4. Xylene separations
Separation of xylene isomers remains a challenging process due to the similar chemical and physical properties of para-xylene \((p\mathsf{X})\) , meta-xylene \((m\mathsf{X})\) , and ortho-xylene \((o\mathsf{X})\) .[62] These isomers are important chemical feedstock precursors. Namely, \(p\mathsf{X}\) is the most valuable as the raw material for many plastics, fibers, and textiles.[63] As a result, development of energy-efficient methods towards the separation and purification of xylene isomers is crucial and has been labelled as one of the seven chemical separations to change the world.[61]
Conventional distillation methods are not feasible for xylenes separation due to their similar boiling points of \(138.4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , \(139.2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , and \(144.5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(p\mathsf{X}\) , \(m\mathsf{X}\) , and \(o\mathsf{X}\) , respectively. [64] Therefore, separation of these isomers is either performed through fractional
## MINIREVIEW
crystallization or adsorption through simulated moving beds.[65] However, both of these methods require high energy input for heating or cooling. Therefore, the development of physical adsorbents that can operate near room temperature and ambient conditions is essential for reducing the carbon footprint of this separation.
MOFs have been proposed as possible candidates for these separations; however, the flexibility arising from local linker rotation of 2D-linkers and global structural breathing hinders their full potential in xylenes separation. The kinetic diameters of
xylenes \((p\mathsf{X}=6.7\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}},\,m\mathsf{X}=7.1\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}},\,o\mathsf{X}=7.4\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}})\) require sub-angstrom precision and control of pore size and aperture to achieve significant separation selectivity. Farha and co-workers (2022) demonstrated that sub-angstrom control cannot be achieved using 2D-linkers due to linker rotation which can alter the pore size compared to the negligible difference observed in 3D-LMOFs.[14] Furthermore, the incorporation of 3D-linker into the framework increased the rigidity of the framework, thus allowing it to maintain full porosity after activation.
Farha and co-workers (2022) demonstrated the differentiation of xylene isomers in a series of MOFs with isostructural analogues of MIL-47/MIL-53. Two MOFs were formed using the 3D-linker (Figure 11b), bodc, [Al(bodc)(OH)] (NU-2000) (10) (Figure 11a) and \([V_{2}(b o d c)_{2}(O H)_{2}]{:}0.5H_{2}b o d c\) (NU-2001) (11). Single-component xylene isotherms were collected to show the uptake of each isomer at room temperature for 10, 11 and MIL-53 for comparison (Figure 11d). Interestingly, the 3DL-MOFs showed a substantial differentiation between the isomers. Specifically, nearsieving separation can be achieved between \(p\mathsf{X}\) and oX in 10 and 11. In contrast, MIL-53 showed similar uptakes for all isomers under the same conditions. Furthermore, similar trends were observed in the multi-component liquid batch uptake experiment and corroborated with GCMC simulations (Figure 11e).
Yang and co-workers (2022) further demonstrated this strategy using 8 to realize the precise differentiation of xylene isomers through formation of nonaromatic confined pores using multiple 3D-linkers.[66] This MOF exhibited significant separation of xylene isomers and ethyl benzene in a quaternary breakthrough test at 423 K, while its 2D counterpart, \([{\mathsf{C u}}({\mathsf{b d c}})({\mathsf{t e d}})_{1/2}]\) , showed no obvious separation selectivity, further corroborating that subangstrom level control can be employed using 3D-linkers.
## 3.5. Krypton/Xenon separations
Another challenging endeavor is the separation of krypton (Kr) from xenon (Xe) due to their similar kinetic diameters (3.65 Å and 4.05 Å, respectively) and their similar chemical properties.[44] Noble gases like Kr and Xe are important in many applications including the lighting, semiconductor, medical, and space industries.[38, 67] These gases are often found naturally in mixtures with other gases or in used nuclear fuel (UNF) reprocessing offgas streams. In industry, Kr and Xe are commonly separated via cryogenic distillation; however, this separation is costly and energy intensive, as temperatures of \(-110\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) or lower are necessary.[67]
Yang and co-workers extended their analysis of ZUR-C1 (5) and ZUR-C2 (6) for Kr/Xe separation in addition to \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}/\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{8}\) separation, as Xe and \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}\) have similar kinetic
## MINIREVIEW
diameters (4.05 Å and 4.44 Å, respectively).[15] Compared to Kr, a steep uptake of Xe was observed at low pressures which plateaued at 2.05 mmol \(\mathfrak{g}^{-1}\) in single component adsorption isotherms for 6 (Figure 12a). At ultra-low Xe uptake in 6, they calculated high \(\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}}\) values reaching \(28\,\mathsf{\ k J\ m o l}^{-1}\) and a Xe/Kr Henry's selectivity of 19.0, marking 6 as a good candidate for ultralow concentration Kr/Xe separation. The pore confinement effect, resulting from incorporation of the cdc linker, contributes to higher binding energies between Xe and framework O-atoms. While 5 adsorbed Xe more readily than Kr, Xe adsorption was not as steep at low pressures as was seen in 6 and the MOF only exhibited a Henry's selectivity of 8.7. Breakthrough experiments were conducted under dilute Xe and Kr conditions to simulate UNF reprocessing conditions (Figure 12b-c). The breakthrough experiments showed that Kr was not retained in 6, while the retention time for Xe was \(222\,\mathsf{m i n}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\).
